  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Childhood Vaccine Hesitancy (2026) in New England Journal of Medicine. 2 news article(s) about the work (Undark Magazine, Nature).</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Childhood Vaccine Hesitancy (2026) in New England Journal of Medicine is authored by Sean T. O\u2019Leary and Margie Danchin. Topics: Medicine, Vaccination, Family medicine, Vaccine safety and Incidence (geometry).</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">7 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include Acta Paediatrica, Current Opinion in Pediatrics and Current Pediatrics Reports.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This NEJM article on childhood vaccine hesitancy shows an early but clear uptake signal: it has 7 citations and has already been reused in 8 downstream records. Its influence is concentrated in pediatrics, vaccination, and vaccine-communication scholarship, with citations appearing in specialty journals that address child health, immunization, infectious diseases, and misinformation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">\u201cChildhood Vaccine Hesitancy\u201d presents a clinically oriented synthesis of a persistent public health problem: most hesitant parents are not rejecting vaccination outright, but are worried about safety and want to protect their children. The article emphasizes that routine childhood vaccines have substantially reduced disease incidence and retain a strong safety record, and it foregrounds the role of clinicians as the most trusted source of vaccine information. That framing places the paper squarely at the intersection of pediatrics, family medicine, risk communication, and vaccine safety.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record suggests that the article is already serving as a reference point for work on vaccine coverage and hesitancy. Citations are still few in number, so the signal should be read as emerging rather than settled, but the pattern is coherent: the work is being taken up in pediatric and infectious-disease venues rather than diffuse generalist settings. The citing literature includes articles in Acta Paediatrica, Current Opinion in Pediatrics, Current Pediatrics Reports, Microorganisms, Medico e Bambino, Clinical Microbiology and Infection, and the New England Journal of Medicine, indicating recognition across clinically relevant journals.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The downstream concepts attached to the citing works reinforce the article\u2019s thematic reach. The most common link is to vaccine coverage and hesitancy, with additional connections to immune responses and vaccinations, virology, and misinformation. This suggests that the paper is being used not only as a discussion of parental decision-making, but also as part of broader conversations about immunization uptake, disease prevention, and communication strategies in an era of persistent vaccine uncertainty.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citation, the record shows documented reuse and modest public-facing attention. There are 8 reused-by records, including one Europe PMC biomedical article mention, and one podcast mention from TWIV. The public-discourse archive is unavailable, so the record cannot support a broader claim about online conversation; what it does show is that the article has already entered specialist commentary and a small but growing scholarly network. Overall, the impact profile is that of a high-visibility, clinically relevant piece whose influence is beginning to accumulate in the literature on childhood vaccination.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
